\begin{theorem}[Tate--sieve vector]
For $\phi_{p,\tau}=\mathbf 1_{\Z_p^\times}+p^{-\tau}\mathbf 1_{p\Z_p}$: $Z_p(\phi_{p,\tau},s)=\int_{\Q_p^\times}\phi_{p,\tau}|x|^s_pd^\times x=1+\frac{p^{-s-\tau}}{1-p^{-s}}$, so $\prod_{p\in S}Z_p=\sum_{p|n\Rightarrow p\in S}n^{-s}\operatorname{rad}(n)^{-\tau}$; $Z_p(\hat\phi_{p,\tau},1-s)=\gamma_p(s)Z_p(\phi_{p,\tau},s)$ with $\gamma_p(s)=\frac{1-p^{-s}}{1-p^{s-1}}$ independent of $\tau$; $\hat\phi_{p,\tau}=e_0+(p^{-\tau}-1)p^{-1}e_{-1}$ (self-dual iff $\tau=0$), and with $E_p,O_p$ the Fourier-even/odd parts $\hat\phi-\phi=\tau(\log p)O_p+O(\tau^2)$. The Fourier transform and shift on the lattice line $\mathcal R_p=\operatorname{span}\{f_k=\mathbf 1_{p^k\Z_p}\}$ satisfy $\mathcal F^2=1$, $\mathcal FU\mathcal F=U^{-1}$ (a $D_\infty$-representation), and $\mathcal R_p$ is the smallest Fourier- and shift-stable space containing $\phi_{p,\tau}$, $\tau\ne0$. The family $(\phi_{p,\tau})_p$, $\tau\ne0$, is not a pure tensor in the restricted tensor product with basic vectors $\mathbf 1_{\Z_p}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{status}Theorem (classical; Tate's thesis \cite{Tate1950}; $\hat e_k=p^{-k}e_{-k}$).\end{status}

